  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nDespite weak upper troughing from time to time over the \nweekend, we should remain dry with some diurnal CU. There will \nlikely be some valley fog early this morning, especially in the \nCT River Valley. It is also not out of the question to have some\non the Midcoast as well as mostly moistening southerly flow \ncontinues.\n\nNear-seasonable temperatures are in store for both today and \nSunday with onshore flow cooling the coast down each afternoon. \nValley fog is once again possible tonight and Sunday night, and \npossibly at the coast as well.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nEarly next week, an omega block pattern establishes across the \nregion as the western ridge amplifies and Atlantic high builds \nnorthward, parking a longwave upper level trough over the East Coast \nthrough at least Thursday. An upper-level low over Hudson Bay will \ndeepen the trough, driving a 995 mb surface low across New England. \nThis is actually a rather deep low for July. Model guidance shows \nnotable spread regarding the surface low track. The GFS/GEFS favors \na weaker, more progressive solution further south, whereas the \nECMWF/EPS depicts a stronger (wetter), closed low further north. A \nsignificant trend since yesterday's guidance has been for the \nsurface low to slow down/occlude over New England Wed night-\nThursday. As such, the end of next week is trending wetter.\n\nIn terms of local impacts, generally expecting a wet few days from \nTuesday-Thursday, but possibly lingering into Friday depending on \nhow quickly the surface low exits offshore. The stage will be set \nfor locally heavy downpours as PWATs surge to 1.5-2.0\", with dew \npoints well into the 60s. We will see lowering heights and \ntemperatures aloft with a bit of a cold pool aloft. This will \nsupport modest instability of up to 1000 J/Kg MUCAPE. Thus, there is \npotential for thunderstorms each day, but especially focused during \nthe afternoon/early evening timeframe when surface instability is \nmaximized. It will be showery in nature and come in a few waves, \nrather than be raining the entire time, thankfully. With that said, \nlarge portions of the forecast area have been abundantly dry this \nmonth, so some soaking rain will be beneficial assuming it is not \nlocally excessive.\n\nDespite the increasing humidity, should have seasonable if not \nslightly cool temperatures, given the increased cloud cover and \nlowering 500 mb heights/thickness values by midweek.",
